Render the page's structure as Markdown:
- A heading or title on the page becomes a Markdown heading (`##` or deeper; never `#`).
- Bullet or dash lists become `-` lists, numbered lists stay numbered, indentation is kept as nesting.
- Checkboxes become `- [ ]`, ticked or crossed-off ones `- [x]`.
- Underlined or boxed words become **bold**.
- Crossed-out words are left out.
- Arrows between items become `→`.
- Margin notes go where they best fit, in *italics*.
- Tables become Markdown tables.
- Maths becomes LaTeX: `$...$` inline, `$$...$$` on its own line for a displayed formula.

Lines wrap only because the page ran out of width, so join the lines of a
paragraph or list item into one line. Keep a line break only where the writer
started a new line on purpose: headings, list items, and short lines that stand
on their own.

When a word cannot be read, write `[illegible]`. If you are unsure of a word but
have a good guess, write it followed by `[?]`; do this especially for names,
numbers and technical terms, where a plausible-looking guess is easy to miss.
Never invent text to fill a gap.
For a drawing or diagram, write `[sketch: <a short description>]` and transcribe
any words inside it.
